<article class="env-exercise" id="02EP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02EP">Exercise <span data-tag="02EP">111.29.2</span>.</a> For $f \in {\mathbf Z}[x, u]$ we define $f_ p(x) = f(x, x^ p) \bmod p \in {\mathbf F}_ p[x]$. Give an example of an $f \in {\mathbf Z}[x, u]$ such that the following two properties hold: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
There exist infinitely many $p$ such that $f_ p$ does not have a zero in ${\mathbf F}_ p$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For all $p >> 0$ the polynomial $f_ p$ either has a linear or a quadratic factor. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>